# Design system — Sleek Paperstyle

This is the agent-facing description of the logandaim.at visual identity. It is a rulebook, not a
history: follow it literally when building UI. Values live in
[`frontend/src/app/globals.css`](../../frontend/src/app/globals.css) and nowhere else. This file
names tokens and states rules; it never restates hex codes.

**Scope: appearance only.** This document does not describe what the product does, which journeys
it supports, or how data flows through it. It describes how any screen must look, feel, and behave
visually. What is shown inside a component is content, and content decisions live in the product
specs.

## The identity

**Sleek Paperstyle.** A warm, tactile logbook on paper, rendered with the speed and precision of a
modern web application. The interface borrows the material language of uncoated paper and pencil
ruling, and nothing else.

- **Restrained skeuomorphism.** Flat, light paper surfaces, hairline rules, an organic warmth, and
  generous space to breathe. No leather textures, no embossing, no 3D book spines, no ornament.
- **Utility first.** Every interaction is fast, accessible, and logical. The aesthetic never
  competes with reading or entry.
- **Calm focus.** The surface stays quiet so content can be the loudest thing on the screen.

## Do / Don't

The table below is the shortest complete statement of the identity. When a rule elsewhere in this
document and a row here disagree, this table wins.

| ✅ DO | ❌ DON'T |
|-------|----------|
| Use `--bg-surface` (`#FDFBF7`) and `--bg-canvas` (`#F4F1EA`) for a warm, easy-on-the-eye paper atmosphere. | Never use cold tech white as the global page background, and never a blue-cast grey (e.g. Tailwind Slate) as a neutral. |
| Use `--color-primary` (`#1B3B2B`, hunter green) as the single dominant accent colour. | Add no ornamental secondary accents: no brown buttons, no gold gradients, no arbitrarily multi-coloured badges. |
| Reserve `--semantic-impact` (`#A82B2B`) exclusively for impacts, critical errors, and destructive actions. | Never use red for ordinary UI decoration or for neutral diagram elements. |
| Keep borders thin (1px) and corners nearly square (4–6px). | No strong drop shadows, no glassmorphism (`backdrop-blur`), no heavily rounded cards (16px+). |
| Pair serif headings with clear sans-serif forms. | Never use handwriting, script, or cursive faces for user input — legibility always wins. |
| Treat `--semantic-*` as meaning: a colour appears only when something happened. | Never use a semantic colour because a screen looked empty or dull. |

## Tokens

The single source is the block in `frontend/src/app/globals.css`. Every token below is defined for
light and dark. Add a token only together with an entry here.

The canonical token names are the design system's own vocabulary. The `@theme inline` block
republishes them as Tailwind utilities, which is what components actually use.

### Surface & paper (neutrals)

| Token | Utility | Meaning | Use for |
|----|----|----|----|
| `--bg-canvas` | `bg-background` | The desk the paper lies on | App shell and page bodies, sidebar |
| `--bg-surface` | `bg-surface` | A sheet of paper | Cards, tables, content areas, header |
| `--bg-elevated` | `bg-surface-elevated` | A raised sheet | Active inputs, dropdown menus, dialogs |
| `--bg-surface-muted` | `bg-surface-muted` | A second sheet / greyed paper | Table headers, hover rows, skeletons, inactive tracks |
| `--border-subtle` | `border-border` | Pencil hairline | Dividers, card borders, table rules |
| `--border-strong` | `border-border-strong` | A field's contour | Form inputs at rest, millimetre grid |

### Typography & ink

| Token | Utility | Meaning | Use for |
|----|----|----|----|
| `--text-primary` | `text-foreground` | Warm deep ink (`#2C2A29`); never pure black | Body text, headings, default icons |
| `--text-secondary` | `text-muted-foreground` | Secondary ink | Labels, subtitles, metadata, units |
| `--text-muted` | `text-placeholder` | Muted ink | Placeholders and disabled elements only |

### The one accent

| Token | Utility | Meaning | Use for |
|----|----|----|----|
| `--color-primary` | `bg-primary`, `text-primary`, `border-primary` | **The one brand colour: hunter green** | One primary button per screen, active nav, focus rings, neutral vector drawings |
| `--color-primary-hover` | `bg-primary-hover` | The pressed primary | Hover and active state of a primary button |
| `--color-primary-subtle` | `bg-primary-subtle` | A green wash | Subtle badges, selected table rows |
| `--primary-foreground` | `text-primary-foreground` | Paper on hunter green | Labels drawn on `--color-primary` |

### Semantic & data colours (meaning only)

| Token | Utility | Meaning | Use for |
|----|----|----|----|
| `--semantic-impact` | `bg-destructive`, `text-destructive` | Crimson | Impact points, critical deviations, delete actions, errors |
| `--semantic-warning` | `bg-warning`, `text-warning` | Ocher / amber | Warnings, unsaved changes, moderate bands |
| `--semantic-success` | `bg-success`, `text-success` | Forest green | Optimal band, confirmations, positive trends |
| `--semantic-info` | `bg-info`, `text-info` | Steel blue | Meteorological data and distance helper lines only |

### Mechanics

| Token | Meaning | Use for |
|----|----|----|
| `--contour` | Alias of the hairline | Tick rules (`rule-ticks`), quiet sheet rules |
| `--color-secondary` | Neutral paper fill | Inactive tracks, muted chips, segmented rest state |
| `--color-ring` | Focus indicator | Every `focus-visible` outline |
| `--radius` | Corner radius | `--radius-sm` through `--radius-xl` are derived from it |
| `--shadow-paper` | Sheet shadow | The `paper` class. Soft in light, `none` in dark |
| `--shadow-overlay` | Floating-sheet shadow | The `paper-overlay` class: dialogs, menus, tooltips |
| `--font-sans` | Interface face: **Inter** | Menus, tables, forms, small figures. Never name a family in a component |
| `--font-serif` | Book face: **Lora** | Titles only — page, card, and dialog headings |
| `--font-mono` | Machine values only | References, base URLs, token names, file names. Never for prose |

Inter is the work face and Lora is the book face. Headings use `font-serif`; everything the user
works with uses `font-sans`. Weights are listed in `layout.tsx` — add one there before using a
`font-*` weight that is not loaded, or the browser will synthesise it. Geist Mono is the machine
face.

Dark mode is class-based (`@custom-variant dark`), with `prefers-color-scheme` as the default. The
gallery sets `.light` and `.dark` on wrappers to show both themes side by side.

## Colour discipline

There is exactly **one brand colour**. Everything else is paper, ink, or an explicit meaning:

- Hunter green: brand, primary action, active state. Nothing else.
- Paper and warm neutrals: all structure.
- Impact, warning, success, info: meaning only, never decoration. Always paired with a word.

`--semantic-impact` is the most restricted colour in the system. It means "this is an impact", "this
failed", or "this destroys something", and nothing else. It is never a hover accent, a chart series,
a category marker, or a way to make a screen feel lively. `--semantic-info` is reserved for
meteorological data and distance helper lines; it is not a general-purpose "informational" accent.

## Contrast exceptions

Four prescribed values do not clear WCAG 2.2 AA as text. They are kept because they are prescribed,
and each is restricted to the role where it is legible:

| Value | Ratio on paper | Restriction |
|----|----|----|
| `--text-muted` `#9E9A93` | 2.48:1 | Placeholders and disabled controls only. Never body text, labels, or metadata. |
| `--semantic-warning` `#D97706` | 3.08:1 | Rules, washes, icons, and borders. A warning that must be *read* is ink on the amber wash. |
| `--border-strong` `#CFCAC0` | 1.58:1 | A 1px contour, never a text carrier. |
| `--semantic-success` `#15803D` | 4.45:1 on canvas, 4.85:1 on surface | Text on `--bg-surface` only, never on the darker canvas. |

The rule this implies: **a semantic colour may always be a mark, a rule, or a wash; it may be text
only where the table above allows it.** Badge and Alert already encode this — `warning` is ink on an
amber wash rather than amber text.

## Paper language

Six elements carry the identity. Use them; do not invent a seventh.

1. **The sheet.** Content sits on `Card` — a hairline and a whisper of shadow, never a heavy border
   or an elevation card. In dark mode the shadow is `none` and the hairline alone separates the
   sheet.
2. **The serif title.** Page, card, and dialog titles are `font-serif`. The serif is the book face
   and the only thing that carries the material character; body text never uses it.
3. **The ruled line.** `Separator` draws `rule-ticks`: small ticks along a hairline, like the
   printed ruling of a form. Sections are divided by a ruled line, never by empty space alone or by
   a shadow.
4. **The fine contour.** Inputs and selects sit on `--bg-elevated` with a 1px `--border-strong`
   contour that turns `--color-primary` on focus and darkens slightly on hover. A card's hairline is
   `--border-subtle`; a field's contour is stronger, because the field boundary is functional.
5. **The stamp.** `Badge` is a rubber stamp: hairline box, tracked small caps, a tint of the
   semantic colour. Status always carries its word.
6. **The millimetre grid.** Charts and diagrams are thin geometric lines on a faint grid, like
   graph paper — drawn from `--contour`, never filled shapes or gradients.

## Rules

1. **Semantic utilities only.** Use `bg-surface`, `text-muted-foreground`, `border-border`,
   `bg-primary`, `text-destructive`, and so on. Never write a raw palette class (`bg-stone-100`,
   `text-green-800`, `border-red-200`) or a hex value in a component. `check:design` fails the
   build otherwise. Paper patterns come from `rule-ticks`, `rule-ticks-quiet`, `leader`,
   `select-chevron`, or `checkbox-tick`; their SVG masks live in `globals.css` so they inherit
   `currentColor` instead of hardcoding a stroke colour. An inline style may reference only
   `var(--…)` tokens.
2. **Contrast is a gate.** Text on a background meets WCAG 2.2 AA (4.5:1 normal text). The gallery
   measures every documented pair; a change that drops a pair below AA is not accepted. The four
   prescribed exceptions above are role-restricted rather than relaxed.
3. **Colour is never the only signal.** Status and validation carry their meaning in text or an
   icon too. Badges and alerts always include the word.
4. **Touch targets are at least 44 by 44 pixels** on every interactive element. The target and the
   drawn control are not the same thing: a control keeps its neighbour's height and extends its hit
   area with a `before` overlay. Never make a control taller than its row to reach 44px.
5. **Focus is always visible.** Interactive elements use `focus-visible:outline-2` with
   `outline-ring`. Never remove an outline without an equally visible replacement.
6. **One primary action per screen.** Everything else is `secondary` or `ghost`. Destructive
   actions use `destructive` and are never the primary button.
7. **German UI copy, English code.** Labels are German, identifiers and comments are English. Do
   not hardcode new copy in a component beyond the gallery examples; product wording lives with the
   product.
8. **Spacing scale** is the Tailwind default (4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32 px). Page bodies use `p-6`,
   cards use `p-6`, form fields stack with `space-y-1.5` inside `Field`. White space is part of the
   identity: prefer a wider gap to another rule.
9. **Variants come from `cva`.** New variants are added in the component's `cva` definition, not by
   passing arbitrary classes at call sites. Public variant names stay backwards compatible.
10. **Flat by default.** Edges come from `border-border` and `--contour`. Cards carry the `paper`
    shadow class, which is `none` in dark mode; only overlays that genuinely float (dialogs,
    dropdowns, tooltips) carry `paper-overlay`. No hover elevation, gradients, or glass. A dragging
    row is marked by `ring-1 ring-primary`, never by a shadow.
11. **Angular paper, not pills.** `--radius` is 4–6px: controls use `rounded-sm`, containers
    `rounded-md`. The only pill shapes are where the shape is the meaning (progress track, switch
    track, radio dot). A rounded corner is never decoration.
12. **Serif for titles only.** `font-serif` on page, card, and dialog headings; never on body copy,
    table cells, form labels, or buttons. Never a script or cursive face anywhere — user input must
    stay maximally legible.
13. **Warm and natural, low saturation.** Warm paper and warm ink; dark mode is warm charcoal, never
    pure black and never cold blue-grey. Status colours stay distinguishable without glowing.

## Components

Primitives live in `frontend/src/components/ui/`. They are shadcn-style: `class-variance-authority`
for variants, Radix primitives where a behaviour is non-trivial, `cn` (`clsx` + `tailwind-merge`)
for class merging.

- Only `Button`, `Alert`, and `Badge` carry status variants. Do not invent colour variants for other
  components.
- `Card` is a paper sheet and carries `paper`. `CardTitle` is `font-serif`; `CardHeader` closes with
  a `rule-ticks-quiet` hairline so the title block reads as a printed form head.
- `Badge` is a stamp: hairline, tracked caps, tinted. It names its status in words. `primary` is the
  one stamp in brand ink; use it for the single state worth marking strongly on a screen.
- `Alert` is a margin note: a coloured left rule and a tinted wash, never a full coloured block.
  `error` uses `role="alert"`.
- `Separator` draws the ruled line. It is the standard section divider.
- `Input`, `Textarea`, `Select`, and `SearchInput` sit on `--bg-elevated` with a `--border-strong`
  contour that turns `--color-primary` on focus and darkens on hover. `Select` stays a native
  `<select>` on purpose: the platform picker is the control users know, and it survives touch and
  one-handed use. Its chevron is a mask in `currentColor`, so it flips with the theme.
- `Checkbox` is a square with `checkbox-tick`: green fill and a paper-coloured tick, both from
  tokens. `Switch` and the radio dot are the only pills, because there the shape is the meaning.
- `Table` is a ruled ledger: hairlines between rows, no zebra banding, `tabular-nums` and
  right-aligned figures. `TableKeyValue` renders the label · dotted-leader · value pairs used for
  metric lists.
- `Progress` is a measured band with fifth-ticks on its track; it keeps `rounded-sm`, since a
  progress track's roundness would mean nothing.
- `Slider` is a value input drawn as a ruled measure: a compact squared knob on a divided track,
  with a 44px hit area around the knob. Do not enlarge the visible knob to reach the touch minimum.
- `CheckpointSlider` is the same input for a value scale that has named checkpoints: the track is
  divided into the coloured bands between them. **The band colours are caller-supplied data**, not
  design tokens, and arrive as a `checkpoints` prop; the design system owns no palette for them and
  must never add one to `globals.css`. Callers pass `var(--…)` token references, never hex values.
  The value moves freely and never snaps to a checkpoint.
  Bands are `aria-hidden`; the legend repeats every checkpoint with its name, and the thumb's
  `aria-valuetext` names the nearest one. With no checkpoints it renders a plain track.
- `Table` rows that drag are sortable through `@dnd-kit`. The drag activator is a dedicated handle,
  never the whole row; its hit area follows the touch rule. A handle's accessible name must be
  distinct per row and must not invent a position that does not exist. Numbers in tables are
  `tabular-nums` and right-aligned; machine values are mono. Table heads are tracked small caps on
  `--surface-muted`.
- `Field` owns label, hint, and error placement. Use it instead of hand-assembling label + input.
- Layout components (`app-shell`, `sidebar`, `page-chrome`) and workflow components are composed
  from the primitives. The active nav item is marked by a filled treatment plus `aria-current`,
  never by colour alone.

## Screen composition

A screen is a page of the logbook. **These rules govern appearance only.** How a journey is
structured — whether something is shown as a map, a progress bar, or a plain step list — is a
product decision and is not settled by this identity.

1. The page header is a serif title with a hairline ruled line beneath it.
2. Content blocks are `Card`s (sheets) or ruled table sections; between sections sits a `Separator`.
3. Work is dense and scannable: one primary action, tables for records, forms stacked in `Field`.
4. Numbers in rows: right-aligned, `tabular-nums`. IDs, references, coordinates: `--font-mono`.
5. Exactly one primary action per screen, in the first action row. Everything else secondary/ghost.
6. Status always as word (+ icon or stamp), colour only as second channel.

## The gallery at `/dev`

`frontend/src/app/dev/page.dev.tsx` exists only under `next dev`: `next.config.ts` adds the
`dev.tsx` / `dev.ts` page extensions only in the development phase, so `next build` never emits the
route. `build:frontend` asserts that `out/dev` and the gallery code are absent from the export.

- The page is laid out as a printed specimen book: a cover title block, a table-of-contents index,
  then numbered plates. Each `ComponentSection` carries a two-digit `index` in mono down the
  margin, a serif title, a one-line rationale, and a ruled line before the specimens.
- Every plate renders its content twice — on `.light` (Papier) and `.dark` (Lampe) — so a palette
  change is judged side by side instead of from memory.
- The Tokens plate reads the computed CSS variables and prints the contrast ratio for every
  documented pair, with an AA pass/fail stamp.
- The touch-target toggle outlines interactive elements to reveal targets under 44px. The
  outlines are dashed amber on purpose: they are a measuring overlay, not part of the identity.
- The Datenbilder plate shows the drawing vocabulary as elements — diagram, series, scale, legend —
  deliberately without screens or flows, so the visual language can be judged on its own.
- **The gallery is domain-free.** Its fixtures use neutral German UI language (`Eintrag`, `Status`,
  `Kategorie`, `Fortschritt`, `Alpha`/`Beta`/`Gamma`) and never product entities, shooting terms, or
  a real place name. The gallery demonstrates *how* the product looks; *what* it does is decided in
  the product specs, and a gallery plate must not imply a journey.
- Earlier identity proposals have been removed. The gallery shows the accepted system only; a
  superseded direction belongs in the spec's decision log, not on the page.

Workflow for a visual change: edit `globals.css` or a component, look at `/dev`, decide, then write
the result into this rule and the spec decision log **in the same commit**.

## Keeping this rule in sync

- A new or renamed token, a changed pairing, a new component variant, or a changed visual rule must
  be reflected in this file and in `specs/product/visual-identity.md` in the same commit. There is
  no "document it later".
- `check:design` (part of `mise run check`) fails when a token exists in `globals.css` but is not
  documented here, or when a raw palette class appears under `frontend/src` outside `globals.css`.
- Prose nuance cannot be checked mechanically. The PR checklist item "design rule and spec
  updated" is the human half of the same obligation.
